WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.634
-Our first guest tonight
is an Oscar-, Emmy-,

00:00:02.634 --> 00:00:03.834
and Tony-nominated actor

00:00:03.834 --> 00:00:06.000
you know from films
such as "Marriage Story,"

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.901
"House of Gucci,"
and the "Star Wars" franchise.

00:00:07.901 --> 00:00:10.901
He stars in "65," which is in
theaters Friday, March 10th.

00:00:10.901 --> 00:00:11.934
Please welcome back to the show,

00:00:11.934 --> 00:00:13.434
our friend Adam Driver,
everybody.

00:00:13.434 --> 00:00:16.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.434 --> 00:00:25.601
♪♪

00:00:25.601 --> 00:00:34.767
♪♪

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:36.367
Welcome back!

00:00:36.367 --> 00:00:37.834
-Thank you, thank you,
thank you.

00:00:37.834 --> 00:00:39.033
-Very, very, very happy
to have you here.

00:00:39.033 --> 00:00:41.567
-Thanks for having me.
-This is one of those

00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:44.200
really fun for all ages
blockbusters.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.501
I would recommend people see it
in the theater.

00:00:46.501 --> 00:00:50.234
Do you remember the first time
you saw one of these movies

00:00:50.234 --> 00:00:51.968
with your family
where you got to be a kid

00:00:51.968 --> 00:00:53.634
and go out and see one of these

00:00:53.634 --> 00:00:55.167
really fun things
on a big screen?

00:00:55.167 --> 00:00:58.701
-Yeah, mine was -- I think
"Star Trek" was the first movie.

00:00:58.701 --> 00:01:01.767
The one they go to a planet
and they think it's a god.

00:01:01.767 --> 00:01:03.734
-Oh, I don't remember --
I don't remember --

00:01:03.734 --> 00:01:05.400
I'm glad you kept going,
'cause I'm like,

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.000
"I think they go to a planet
in all of them."

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.334
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:10.634
I think William Shatner
directed it.

00:01:10.634 --> 00:01:11.767
-Okay.
-I think it was like

00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:13.334
Leonard Nimoy did the one
with the whales.

00:01:13.334 --> 00:01:15.434
-Okay, you're right.
Nimoy was the whales.

00:01:15.434 --> 00:01:16.934
-Then after that, I think he
directed the one

00:01:16.934 --> 00:01:19.067
where you go
and they think it's god

00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:20.467
and it turns out
to be a false prophet.

00:01:20.467 --> 00:01:22.033
-Okay, gotcha.

00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:24.467
This seems, like, very
complicated for your first one.

00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:25.934
-That was my first one.

00:01:25.934 --> 00:01:28.100
I think I'm getting the dates
right, but I was --

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.334
that I remember being the
first one I saw in the theater.

00:01:30.334 --> 00:01:31.467
-And do you remember
enjoying it?

00:01:31.467 --> 00:01:33.100
-I mean, yeah,
I had a great time.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.934
I watched the show, "The Next
Generation," with my dad.

00:01:35.934 --> 00:01:39.367
-Oh, that's great.
-So, I was expecting it to be --

00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:40.968
and I knew
the William Shatner show

00:01:40.968 --> 00:01:44.701
'cause they did the -- or the
older one, the original ser--

00:01:44.701 --> 00:01:46.834
So, I was expecting it to be
that, but it wasn't that at all

00:01:46.834 --> 00:01:47.901
so it scared
the [bleep] out of me.

00:01:47.901 --> 00:01:49.000
-[ Chuckles ] Right.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.834
Now, I worry, 'cause,
like, you know,

00:01:50.834 --> 00:01:52.400
this film, you know, I --

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.801
My oldest is almost seven.

00:01:53.801 --> 00:01:56.000
And I feel like
I've done a bad job.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.934
We haven't shown them
enough movies yet,

00:01:57.934 --> 00:02:00.400
and so they scare very easily

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:01.868
'cause I don't think they
understand the stakes.

00:02:01.868 --> 00:02:03.767
We were showing them
the first "Paddington,"

00:02:03.767 --> 00:02:06.767
and there's a moment
where Paddington --

00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:08.834
I hope I'm not ruining it.
-No, I -- No.

00:02:08.834 --> 00:02:10.067
[ Laughter ]

00:02:10.067 --> 00:02:11.601
-There's a scene where
Paddington has --

00:02:11.601 --> 00:02:13.100
they, like, kick him
out of the house,

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.801
and my oldest
was, like, overcome with grief.

00:02:15.801 --> 00:02:17.000
And I realized, "Oh, we haven't

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:18.434
taught them that,
like, Paddington's" --

00:02:18.434 --> 00:02:20.300
I was like, "Paddington's
going to be fine."

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:23.601
-Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.
-So, is this -- I mean,

00:02:23.601 --> 00:02:26.567
is this a film -- have you seen
kids see this new film yet?

00:02:26.567 --> 00:02:27.968
-No. No.

00:02:27.968 --> 00:02:30.968
I invited kids from my building
to the premiere part --

00:02:30.968 --> 00:02:32.434
[ Audience awws ]

00:02:32.434 --> 00:02:34.534
[ Laughter ]

00:02:34.534 --> 00:02:36.501
-He charged them for tickets.

00:02:36.501 --> 00:02:38.033
[ Laughter ]

00:02:38.033 --> 00:02:39.968
-Then I harassed them
the whole time.

00:02:39.968 --> 00:02:41.100
They didn't like it.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.033
And they had a great time.
-That's great.

00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:46.167
-My dad actually recorded --
he showed --

00:02:46.167 --> 00:02:47.501
this is a non-sequitur,

00:02:47.501 --> 00:02:49.534
but he showed me "Old Yeller"
when I was young

00:02:49.534 --> 00:02:51.334
and Old Yeller gets shot.
-Yeah.

00:02:51.334 --> 00:02:53.334
-But they get another dog.
-Yeah.

00:02:53.334 --> 00:02:58.000
-He would record a tape on the
VHS, put the tab over the thing,

00:02:58.000 --> 00:02:59.567
give it to friends of ours,

00:02:59.567 --> 00:03:02.300
but cut the movie...
-That's nice.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:04.334
-...when he shoots the dog
and then give it to people.

00:03:04.334 --> 00:03:06.434
So you never saw --
-I remember being very upset

00:03:06.434 --> 00:03:08.534
the first time
I saw "Old Yeller"

00:03:08.534 --> 00:03:10.434
and taking it out on my parents
for not...

00:03:10.434 --> 00:03:12.734
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-And I remember --

00:03:12.734 --> 00:03:15.734
You know, my dad was making a
point, but you know the story is

00:03:15.734 --> 00:03:18.767
about that, like, one, you can
replace a dog and you move on.

00:03:18.767 --> 00:03:19.901
-Right, right.
-And I was like,

00:03:19.901 --> 00:03:22.300
"Yeah, but they didn't
have to shoot it."

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:23.701
-Right, right, right.
-There's another version

00:03:23.701 --> 00:03:25.701
where the dog just gets old.
-Right, right, right. Yeah.

00:03:25.701 --> 00:03:27.467
They didn't have to go so hard
with the rabies, right?

00:03:27.467 --> 00:03:30.601
Doesn't it get rabies?
-They went hard in rabies, yeah.

00:03:30.601 --> 00:03:32.968
[ Laughter ]
-It was a trope at the time.

00:03:32.968 --> 00:03:35.334
-Yeah, rabies was --
Remember simpler times?

00:03:35.334 --> 00:03:36.534
When you were a kid,

00:03:36.534 --> 00:03:37.634
you were just worried about,
like, rabies.

00:03:37.634 --> 00:03:39.000
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.801
Actually, in this movie
we have a quicksand sequence,

00:03:41.801 --> 00:03:44.133
which I remember --
which maybe I shouldn't say.

00:03:44.133 --> 00:03:47.601
But when I was a kid,
there was quicksand everywhere.

00:03:47.601 --> 00:03:50.133
Quicksand was very popular.
-Yeah, no. I think you're right.

00:03:50.133 --> 00:03:51.868
-So much to the point
I was worried

00:03:51.868 --> 00:03:53.868
that I had to worry
about quicksand when I...

00:03:53.868 --> 00:03:56.067
-And I'm guessing
you didn't grow up in a place

00:03:56.067 --> 00:03:57.067
with a lot of quicksand.

00:03:57.067 --> 00:03:58.334
-No, no, no. Indiana.

00:03:58.334 --> 00:03:59.868
-Yeah.
-No quicksand.

00:03:59.868 --> 00:04:03.133
-I think they're at almost zero
deaths a year from quicksand.

00:04:03.133 --> 00:04:04.868
-[ Laughs ] Yeah, yeah.

00:04:04.868 --> 00:04:08.100
-Was that -- When you shoot
a quicksand scene, is it --

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:09.834
I mean, obviously it's not
practical quicksand,

00:04:09.834 --> 00:04:11.968
but I'm assuming
you can't CGI quicksand.

00:04:11.968 --> 00:04:13.234
You've got to be in something.

00:04:13.234 --> 00:04:15.300
-Yeah, they put, like,
a bunch of cork particles.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:16.534
That's what they fill --
-Oh, interesting.

00:04:16.534 --> 00:04:18.501
-But it's very nice.
It's, like, very heated.

00:04:18.501 --> 00:04:19.701
-Oh.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:19.701 --> 00:04:20.834
So, you're saying this might be,

00:04:20.834 --> 00:04:22.767
10 years from now,
a spa treatment?

00:04:22.767 --> 00:04:24.067
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:24.067 --> 00:04:27.767
Yeah, it's the quicksand
close-to-death spa treatment.

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:31.868
-Yeah, I did a quicksand rinse,
and I look and feel great.

00:04:31.868 --> 00:04:32.901
-Yeah.

00:04:32.901 --> 00:04:34.634
My hair is different.
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:34.634 --> 00:04:37.100
-You know, "Jurassic Park,"

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.234
was that a movie you saw
when you were...?

00:04:38.234 --> 00:04:39.367
-Oh, yeah.
"Jurassic Park" was a big one.

00:04:39.367 --> 00:04:40.567
-That's fun.
And I think, like,

00:04:40.567 --> 00:04:42.167
dinosaurs in general,
because it's like --

00:04:42.167 --> 00:04:43.367
You mentioned you watched
"Star Trek"

00:04:43.367 --> 00:04:45.834
before you went to see
"Star Trek," the TV show.

00:04:45.834 --> 00:04:48.434
I think the nice thing for kids
is, like, they all --

00:04:48.434 --> 00:04:50.801
like, you don't have to
introduce dinosaurs to them.

00:04:50.801 --> 00:04:52.167
Like, they just know dinosaurs.

00:04:52.167 --> 00:04:53.834
It's so funny how much my kids
will just come home

00:04:53.834 --> 00:04:55.300
and tell me about
a new dinosaur,

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.100
and I remember the same thing,
and I'm like, "When does...?"

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:59.000
Like, at some point, just other
kids on the playground

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.167
are like, "Hey."
-Right. Right.

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:03.667
-"I wanna fill -- There's
a lot of different dinosaurs."

00:05:03.667 --> 00:05:05.400
-That actually was going on
during the --

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.868
I got this during the first --
the first part of the pandemic

00:05:08.868 --> 00:05:11.000
when everyone was isolated.
-Yeah.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:13.501
-And so I was with my family
all the time,

00:05:13.501 --> 00:05:16.801
and my son was just telling me
everything about every dinosaur.

00:05:16.801 --> 00:05:18.601
I knew the basics -- T. rex

00:05:18.601 --> 00:05:20.133
and everything that was
in "Jurassic Park."

00:05:20.133 --> 00:05:22.267
But apart from that --
So, when I got this...

00:05:22.267 --> 00:05:26.234
And obviously the themes are
obvious in relationship to COVID

00:05:26.234 --> 00:05:28.734
that everyone's kind of facing
this thing that they don't know.

00:05:28.734 --> 00:05:31.667
It's this invisible kind of
thing, but it was also big.

00:05:31.667 --> 00:05:34.567
It was like dinosaurs and laser
guns, and he's into dinosaurs.

00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:36.467
I'm like, "I'm in." You know?
-Yeah.

00:05:36.467 --> 00:05:38.634
-And then we went,
and he kind of --

00:05:38.634 --> 00:05:39.934
he kind of knew everything.

00:05:39.934 --> 00:05:41.601
The guy was,
like, introducing, like,

00:05:41.601 --> 00:05:43.334
"Oh, this is going to be --
might be a little scary.

00:05:43.334 --> 00:05:44.701
Like, don't be scared."
He's like, "No."

00:05:44.701 --> 00:05:46.734
He's like, "Gallimimus. Paleo--"

00:05:46.734 --> 00:05:49.300
He was -- You know,
"An eoraptor."

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:52.100
He was naming everything that --
He was really into it.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:53.868
But he doesn't want to see the
movie 'cause he's going to be --

00:05:53.868 --> 00:05:55.234
it's too scary.
-Really?

00:05:55.234 --> 00:05:57.734
-So, I basically made this thing
for him to watch

00:05:57.734 --> 00:05:58.968
that he has no interest
in watching.

00:05:58.968 --> 00:06:00.367
-Are you --
[ Laughter ]

00:06:00.367 --> 00:06:02.667
Is any part of you worried
that the "I'm too scared"

00:06:02.667 --> 00:06:05.067
is just a cover for the fact

00:06:05.067 --> 00:06:07.100
that he's not a huge fan
of yours as an actor?

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.734
-Yeah. Yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:08.734 --> 00:06:12.367
I tell him, like, "Oh, we're
gonna do movies sometimes."

00:06:12.367 --> 00:06:14.000
Like, "Oh, that's cool.
I hate movies."

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:15.968
[ Laughter ]
He hates movies.

00:06:15.968 --> 00:06:18.200
All of his friends watch --
-So, is it just --

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:19.968
Like, does he just get anxiety
being in a big room?

00:06:19.968 --> 00:06:21.634
-He hates them.

00:06:21.634 --> 00:06:24.100
We showed -- I finally
showed him "Mary Poppins"

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:26.767
as the first movie, and
he's like, "Eh, it was okay."

00:06:26.767 --> 00:06:28.901
We're like, "Do you want to
watch anything else?"

00:06:28.901 --> 00:06:30.367
He's like,
"No, I'm not interested."

00:06:30.367 --> 00:06:33.634
-We also showed our kids
"Mary Poppins" during --

00:06:33.634 --> 00:06:35.934
Can I make...
-This is the original one.

00:06:35.934 --> 00:06:38.634
-Yeah. The original for me.
Can I make an unpopular opinion?

00:06:38.634 --> 00:06:40.133
-Yeah. Yeah.
-Could have used a cut. Right?

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:41.200
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:42.667
-"Mary Poppins"
is a little bit --

00:06:42.667 --> 00:06:44.634
There's stuff you don't remember
from "Mary Poppins."

00:06:44.634 --> 00:06:47.033
-It's pretty wild,
"Mary Poppins."

00:06:47.033 --> 00:06:49.234
-It's pretty wild.
-The whole sequence when

00:06:49.234 --> 00:06:51.734
the guy is laughing and he goes
up to the top of the thing.

00:06:51.734 --> 00:06:54.300
-Yeah. It's terrifying for kids.
-Yeah, yeah. Or adults.

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:57.300
-It's very anti-laughter.
-Yeah, yeah. Yeah, yeah.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:58.567
-Alright.
I've got a ton more questions.

00:06:58.567 --> 00:07:00.634
We'll be right back
with more from Adam Driver.

